Your implementation should adhere to the following constraints:
- The `CustomList` class should extend the `AbstractList` class.
- The `CustomList` class should provide an implementation for the `getHeadDefault()` method.
- The `getHeadDefault()` method should return the default value for the head of the custom list as per the defined criteria.

Write the `CustomList` class with the required implementation for the `getHeadDefault()` method.

### Solution:
```java
public class CustomList extends AbstractList {
    private int[] elements;

    public CustomList(int[] elements) {
        this.elements = elements;
    }

    @Override
    public int getHeadDefault() {
        int n = elements.length;
        boolean[] present = new boolean[n + 1];

        for (int element : elements) {
            if (element > 0 && element <= n) {
                present[element] = true;
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            if (!present[i]) {
                return i;
            }
        }

        return n + 1;
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `CustomList` class extends the `AbstractList` class and provides an implementation for the `getHeadDefault()` method. The method initializes a boolean array to track the presence of elements in the list. It then iterates through the elements to mark their presence in the array. Finally, it finds and returns the smallest positive integer that is not present in the list, satisfying the defined criteria.

