### Solution:
```typescript
function memoized(
  _prototype: unknown,
  propertyKey: string,
  descriptor: PropertyDescriptor,
): void {
  if (!descriptor.get) {
    throw new Error(`@memoized can only be applied to property getters!`);
  }
  if (descriptor.set) {
    throw new Error(`@memoized can only be applied to readonly properties!`);
  }

  const originalGetter = descriptor.get;
  const memoizationKey = Symbol(`${propertyKey}_memoized`);

  descriptor.get = function () {
    if (!this[memoizationKey]) {
      this[memoizationKey] = originalGetter.call(this);
    }
    return this[memoizationKey];
  };
}

class MemoizationExample {
  @memoized
  get calculatedValue(): number {
    // Simulated expensive calculation
    return Math.random();
  }
}

const example = new MemoizationExample();

console.log(example.calculatedValue); // Output the calculated value
console.log(example.calculatedValue); // Output the cached value
```

In the solution, the `memoized` decorator function is completed by adding memoization logic to the property getter. The original getter function is stored, and a unique memoization key is created for each property decorated with `@memoized`. When the getter is called, it checks if the memoization key exists on the instance. If not, it calls the original getter and caches the result using the memoization key. Subsequent calls to the getter return the cached result, thus optimizing performance by avoiding redundant calculations.

