System.out.println(queue.offer("one"));   // true
System.out.println(queue.offer("two"));   // true
System.out.println(queue.offer("three")); // false
System.out.println(queue);                // [one, two]
```

### Solution:
```java
import java.util.LinkedList;

class CustomQueue {
    private LinkedList<String> queue;
    private int capacity;

    public CustomQueue(int capacity) {
        this.queue = new LinkedList<>();
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    public boolean offer(String element) {
        if (queue.size() < capacity) {
            queue.offer(element);
            return true;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public String poll() {
        return queue.poll();
    }

    public String peek() {
        return queue.peek();
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return queue.toString();
    }
}
```

In this solution, we create a custom queue data structure using a LinkedList to store the elements. The `offer(element)` operation checks if the queue size is less than the capacity. If it is, the element is added to the queue using `queue.offer(element)` and true is returned. If the queue is at its maximum capacity, false is returned without modifying the queue.

