### Solution:
```typescript
// Define the custom decorator
function LogMethodCalls(target: any, propertyKey: string, descriptor: PropertyDescriptor) {
  const originalMethod = descriptor.value;

  descriptor.value = function (...args: any[]) {
    console.log(`Method ${propertyKey} called with arguments: ${args}`);
    return originalMethod.apply(this, args);
  };

  return descriptor;
}

// Create a sample Angular component
@Component({
  selector: 'app-sample',
  template: '<p>Sample Component</p>',
})
export class SampleComponent {
  // Apply the custom decorator to the method
  @LogMethodCalls
  sampleMethod(arg1: string, arg2: number) {
    console.log(`Inside sampleMethod with arguments: ${arg1}, ${arg2}`);
  }

  // Another method without the decorator
  anotherMethod() {
    console.log('Inside anotherMethod');
  }
}
```

In this solution, we define a custom decorator `LogMethodCalls` that takes the target class, the property key (method name), and the property descriptor as its parameters. Within the decorator, we store the original method, modify the method to log its name and arguments, and then call the original method using `apply`. We then apply this decorator to the `sampleMethod` of the `SampleComponent` class. When `sampleMethod` is called, it logs the method name and its arguments to the console before executing the original method. The `anotherMethod` does not have the decorator and will execute as usual without logging method calls.

