### Problem:
You are tasked with implementing a visitor pattern for a simple bytecode instruction set used in a virtual machine. The bytecode instructions are represented by different classes, each corresponding to a specific operation. The visitor pattern allows you to define new operations to be performed on these bytecode instructions without modifying their classes.

The code snippet provided is a part of the bytecode instruction classes and the visitor pattern implementation. The `visitPushInstruction`, `visitTypedInstruction`, and `visitACONST_NULL` methods are being called within the `accept` method of a bytecode instruction class. These methods are used to accept a visitor and perform the corresponding visit operation.

Your task is to implement the visitor pattern by creating a visitor interface and concrete visitor classes to perform specific operations on the bytecode instructions. Additionally, you need to modify the bytecode instruction classes to accept visitors and invoke the appropriate visit method based on the type of instruction.

Implement the visitor pattern for the given bytecode instruction classes and provide a visitor that performs a specific operation on each type of instruction.

### Solution:
```java
// Visitor interface defining visit methods for each bytecode instruction
interface InstructionVisitor {
    void visitPushInstruction(PushInstruction instruction);
    void visitTypedInstruction(TypedInstruction instruction);
    void visitACONST_NULL(ACONST_NULL instruction);
    // Add visit methods for other bytecode instructions as needed
}

// Concrete visitor implementing the InstructionVisitor interface
class ConcreteInstructionVisitor implements InstructionVisitor {
    @Override
    public void visitPushInstruction(PushInstruction instruction) {
        // Perform specific operation for PushInstruction
        // Example: Print the value being pushed onto the stack
        System.out.println("Pushing value: " + instruction.getValue());
    }

    @Override
    public void visitTypedInstruction(TypedInstruction instruction) {
        // Perform specific operation for TypedInstruction
        // Example: Validate the type of the instruction
        System.out.println("Validating type: " + instruction.getType());
    }

    @Override
    public void visitACONST_NULL(ACONST_NULL instruction) {
        // Perform specific operation for ACONST_NULL instruction
        // Example: Handle null value in the bytecode
        System.out.println("Handling ACONST_NULL instruction");
    }
    // Implement visit methods for other bytecode instructions as needed
}

// Bytecode instruction classes modified to accept visitors and invoke visit methods
abstract class BytecodeInstruction {
    abstract void accept(InstructionVisitor visitor);
}

class PushInstruction extends BytecodeInstruction {
    private int value;

    public int getValue() {
        return value;
    }

    @Override
    void accept(InstructionVisitor visitor) {
        visitor.visitPushInstruction(this);
    }
}

class TypedInstruction extends BytecodeInstruction {
    private String type;

    public String getType() {
        return type;
    }

    @Override
    void accept(InstructionVisitor visitor) {
        visitor.visitTypedInstruction(this);
    }
}

class ACONST_NULL extends BytecodeInstruction {
    @Override
    void accept(InstructionVisitor visitor) {
        visitor.visitACONST_NULL(this);
    }
}
// Other bytecode instruction classes can be similarly modified

// Usage example
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ConcreteInstructionVisitor visitor = new ConcreteInstructionVisitor();

        BytecodeInstruction pushInstruction = new PushInstruction();
        pushInstruction.accept(visitor);

        BytecodeInstruction typedInstruction = new TypedInstruction();
        typedInstruction.accept(visitor);

        BytecodeInstruction aconstNullInstruction = new ACONST_NULL();
        aconstNullInstruction.accept(visitor);
    }
}
```

In this solution, we define a visitor interface `InstructionVisitor` with visit methods for each type of bytecode instruction. We then create a concrete visitor `ConcreteInstructionVisitor` that implements the `InstructionVisitor` interface and provides specific operations for each type of instruction.

The bytecode instruction classes are modified to accept visitors and invoke the appropriate visit method based on their type. Finally, we demonstrate the usage of the visitor pattern by creating instances of bytecode instructions and accepting the visitor to perform specific operations on them.

